@model ReformedHistory.Views.Content.Confessions.Secret.SecretSection

<h2 id="@this.Model.Link">Chapter @this.Model.Index</h2>
<h3>@this.Model.Name</h3>
<br />

<div class="centerQuote">
    <span>
        Those who say that infancy has nothing in it for Jesus to save are denying that Christ is Jesus for 
        all believing infants... Yes, they're infants, 
        but they are His members. They're infants, but they receive His sacraments. They're infants, but they 
        share in His table in order to have life in themselves.
    </span>
    <span>Saint Augustine, Sermon No. 174</span>
</div>
    
<ol>
    <li>
        Just as God's promises to Abraham were to him and his children, Saint Peter preaches that God's promises
        in Christ are to believers and their children.
    </li>
    <li>
        Because faith is a gift of God along with all other saving graces, God fulfils his promise to the children
        of believers by granting them an infant faith appropriate to their 
        age and maturity.
    </li>
    <li>
        Infant faith is a covenantal bond of trust between the child and God that is not dependent on intellectual
        or linguistic ability, much like the bond between infants and their parents. Scripture
        testifies to this bond through David, 
        <i>
            "For You, O Lord, are my hope, 
            my trust, O Lord, from my youth. 
            Upon You I have leaned from before my birth;
            You are He who took me from my mother's womb.
            My praise is continually of You."
        </i>
    </li>
    <li>
        Infant faith is effectual for salvation and the reception of grace in the sacraments.
        However, it is also dependent 
        on the covenant community to grow into a full, adult faith. The parents and
        their church have a holy responsibility to nurture this faith and raise the child
        in the fear and admonition of the Lord.
    </li>
    <li>
        Covenant infants are not to be excluded from the sacraments, but are to be baptized as members in Christ's
        church and receive the Lord's supper as members of Christ's body. Through their infant faith, 
        covenant children are grafted into Christ and grow to inherit the fullness of the promises given 
        to their parents.
    </li>
</ol>
<br />